WEBVTT

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.320
I'm Hakimadi, professor of the history of
Africa and the African

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.100
diaspora at the University

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.720
of Chitistah in Sussex.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.140
The inspiration behind my work is many
things, I

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.510
suppose growing up down

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.710
here in Kent a long

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.810
time ago when, you

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.010
know, there wasn't any history, certainly
when I went to school there

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:49.250
wasn't

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.210
any history which reflected all about
anybody you look like me or

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:58.770
look like you. So that

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:05.650
was a big concern of mine, especially as a
teenager. And

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.730
of course going back in those

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:12.910
days you didn't see Africans or people of
African heritage

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.230
on TV to be honest either or

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:20.470
anywhere very much. So it was as if, you
know, we

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.730
didn't exist. Plus, it was a time of,

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:27.450
you know,

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.210
quite a lot of racism and all kinds of
things were going on. So as

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.530
a young person growing up,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:41.210
I was very conscious that I was living in
a world which, you

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.890
know, discriminated against me or

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.930
discriminated against people like me and
where we weren't

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:53.750
represented. So when I was about I guess

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:59.570
a teenager, probably about 13, I decided I
need to start investigating

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:00.690
these things from myself

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.710
finding out, you know, where is this
history, why

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.150
doesn't it appear anywhere, why isn't it
in school,

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.350
why isn't it on TV, why doesn't I want to
talk about it. So I just

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.990
began to find books and

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.270
Go to, you know, Canterbury, I think it
was a bookshop, you know,

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:20.830
you know, a bookshop

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:21.810
linked to the university.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:27.230
So I could just go and look for books,
anything, anything related

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.810
to Africa, it could be a

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.470
novel, it could be history, it could be
anything, all related to

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.770
maybe African -American history,

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:42.250
I just buy them and start investigating
and kind of finding evidence,

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.410
you know, to combat

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:49.350
or to deal with, you know, the kind of
world as it affected me.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:55.390
So that's really where it started, just
investigating, finding

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.050
out things for myself and then I decided

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.990
again at quite the early age that, you
know, wouldn't it be great

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.410
to go to university and

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.950
to study this thing, this history, and
then to become a, I could

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.110
become a schoolteacher,

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.410
I could teach other people, so that they
wouldn't have to go through

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:13.930
what I'd gone through and so on.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.350
And in those days, this is the mid -70s,
there was a beginning of

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.810
people thinking that, yeah,

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.390
these kind of things should be taught in
school. There was a few

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.930
areas of the country, I think,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:30.410
in Birmingham on that stage of the kind of
old level in, you know,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:31.550
whatever African and

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:35.410
cabin history or something, the beginnings
of it. So I think, okay,

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:36.090
I've got a university,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.950
I study African history. When I come out,
I'll be a teacher and I'll

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.070
teach. So that's really

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:47.330
where it started. That was what inspired
me to research,

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.330
to investigate things for myself,

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:54.370
to find out as much information as I
could. What inspired me was

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.330
the situation I found

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:01.390
myself in that to me this history needed
to

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.310
be researched, needed to be told and I was

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:10.270
kind of determined to do it and to do
anyway to try and do it so

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.910
that's what kept me going

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:16.810
and still keeps me going to be honest
because you know there's still

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:17.930
lots of challenges and

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:23.090
difficulties but once you are clear that

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.830
something needs to be done and you're in a
position

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:31.150
to do something about it, then you have to
get on with it and do

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.690
the best. Anyway, that's

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:37.810
the answer to your question. I think what
inspired the fifth pack

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.750
work was, well obviously the

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:45.790
creation of the book, that I kind of

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:50.850
wrote with with Marica, Marica Sherwood,
and that

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.150
was

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:56.430
was essentially coming up to the 50th

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:02.170
anniversary of the 5th back in 1995, so
there was perhaps

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:07.750
the first time when people celebrated the
fact that a

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:09.490
Congress had taken place in Manchester.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:14.310
What did I learn by contributing to the
book on the 5th African Congress?

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:23.530
Well, one thing clearly is the importance
of that Congress because

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:28.090
there were four Congresses

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.770
before that, as well as Pan -African
Conference in London, and there

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:33.650
have been Pan -African

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:37.990
Congresses since, but the fifth Pan
-African Congress in Manchester

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.770
was probably the most

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:44.930
important of all of those congresses. It's
the one

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.970
everybody looks back to today. If you

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:53.330
check out something in the African Union
or something, people will

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.350
talk about the Manchester

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.070
Congress. They won't talk about the Fourth
Congress or the Third

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:58.290
Congress or the Second Congress,

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:03.530
but the Manchester Congress is very
important, partly because of

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:09.730
when it was held at the

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:09.930
end of

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:15.190
system was under threat, where people in
the colonies, especially

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.370
Africa and the Caribbean,

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.090
were kind of flexing their muscles and
saying, look, we're not going

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:19.910
to put up with this

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:24.070
anymore. We want to be free and
independent. So the timing was very

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:27.590
important. Also, because

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:31.250
of some of the personalities involved,
Kwame and crewmer, George

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.030
Padmore, Joe Mulcanyata,

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:39.090
Amy Ashwood Garvey and others and many
many others. So

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.530
that that made it very

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:45.910
important. Then the politics of it, if you
look at that famous photo

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:46.390
of the

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:51.410
Congress, you see all the slogans that
they put up on the walls of

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:52.210
Chorton Town

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:57.350
Hall which shows what they were about,
down with

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:01.810
antisemitism for example, down

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:07.090
down with opposed to the colour bar,
oppressed people

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:11.290
of all countries unite, labour in the

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.930
white skin cannot emancipate itself while
labour in the black skin

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:15.930
is enslaved, which

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:20.230
is a quote from Karl Marx and so on. So
they had a very international

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:21.350
politics, although

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:24.950
they were focusing on Africa and the
diaspora, they were concerned

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.730
about Palestine, they

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.150
were concerned about what was going on in
Asia, they were concerned

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.510
about what the workers

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:35.570
because we're going through in Britain and
so on. And in fact, Padmore,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:36.810
who was the main organizer,

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:43.990
wrote and hoped that the Congress would
inspire workers

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:45.930
and trade unions in Britain to support

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:50.030
them even more because they were planning
a kind of follow -up Congress

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:50.590
in Africa,

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:55.670
perhaps in 1947 that actually never, never
took place. So anyway,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.070
it's important for all of these

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:02.550
reason and if you look at the declarations
the key declarations were

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.610
not only do we

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:08.010
want to be free but for the first time we
will use force if necessary

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.150
we'll

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.790
organize in particularly amongst the
workers amongst the farmers

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:13.550
the masses

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.330
of the people they're the force that's
going to change everything

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.390
that was also

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:22.150
very very important they were very clear
what they were opposed to

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:23.170
the monopoly

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:26.710
properly of capital, the capital -centred
system, the imposition

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.990
of alien political institutions

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:32.990
or the whole political setup which had
been imposed on Africa, the

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:34.450
boundaries, the colonial

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:38.570
boundaries in Africa which they rejected.
So these things which haven't

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:39.170
even yet been

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:44.370
brought into fruition, that's what they
stood for in 1945. So

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:45.490
that politics, that

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:50.210
orientation, very, very important. So for
all of those reasons, it's

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:51.210
very crucial, of

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.770
of course, it happened in Manchester.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.530
And you know originally it was going to be
in Paris,

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.730
but as I always tell people the weather is
much better

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:00.950
in Manchester, so they changed.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:01.970
It's true.
